## Coding Standards

### Language and Naming Conventions

**All code, comments, and documentation must be written in English.** This is a mandatory requirement for all technical artifacts in the project.

- **Component Naming**: Use PascalCase for React components (e.g., `AuthCard`, `LoginForm`, `NavBar`)
- **Variable Naming**: Use camelCase for variables and functions (e.g., `userId`, `handleSubmit`, `fetchUser`)
- **Constants Naming**: Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants (e.g., `API_BASE_URL`, `BCRYPT_ROUNDS`)
- **Type/Interface Naming**: Use PascalCase for types and interfaces (e.g., `SafeUser`, `AuthResponse`, `ButtonProps`)
- **File Naming**: Use PascalCase for component files (e.g., `LoginForm.tsx`, `AuthCard.tsx`) and camelCase for utility files (e.g., `api.ts`, `types.ts`)
- **CSS Class Naming**: Use TailwindCSS utility classes; for custom classes use kebab-case
- **Hook Naming**: Use camelCase starting with "use" prefix (e.g., `useAuth`, `useTheme`)

**Examples:**

```typescript
// Good: All in English
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';
import { useAuth } from '@/hooks/useAuth';

type LoginFormProps = {
  onSuccess?: () => void;
};

export default function LoginForm({ onSuccess }: LoginFormProps) {
  const { login, error } = useAuth();
  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);

  const handleSubmit = async (e: React.FormEvent) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    setIsLoading(true);
    // Handle login
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit} className="flex flex-col gap-4">
      {/* Component JSX */}
    </form>
  );
}

// Avoid: Non-English comments or names
const FormularioLogin = ({ alExito }: { alExito?: () => void }) => {
  const [estaCargando, setEstaCargando] = useState(false);
  // Manejar envío del formulario
};
```

**Error Messages and Console Logs:**

```typescript
// Good: English error messages
catch (error) {
  console.error('Failed to fetch user profile:', error);
  dispatch({ type: 'AUTH_ERROR', payload: 'Unable to load profile. Please try again.' });
}

// Avoid: Non-English messages
catch (error) {
  console.error('Error al obtener perfil:', error);
}
```

### Design System Compliance Rule

Every visual element must follow what is documented in the Design System showcase (`/admin/design-system`). The showcase is the **single source of truth** for all visual implementation.

- **Components**: Use existing `ui/` components (DataTable, Badge, Button, Input, Accordion, Toast, etc.) — never raw HTML equivalents (`<table>`, `<button>`, inline `<span>` badges)
- **Typography**: Use only documented tokens (`text-h1`, `text-h2`, `text-h3`, `text-body`, `text-caption`) — never raw text sizes (`text-sm`, `text-xs`, `text-lg`)
- **Colors**: Use only semantic tokens (`text-content-primary`, `bg-surface-primary`, `border-border-strong`, etc.) — never raw hex/rgba or opacity modifiers on tokens (`text-content-primary/50`)
- **Spacing / Radius / Shadows**: Use documented design tokens from Token Inspector — never arbitrary values without justification
- **Animations**: Follow the Motion Patterns guide in the showcase (CSS `transition` for hover, CSS `@keyframes` for entry, CSS `grid-rows` for accordion, Framer Motion for mount/unmount list items) — never inline `setTimeout` animation hacks
- **Icons**: Use documented sizes (16px inline, 24px medium, 32px page-level) from Token Inspector

**Before implementing any UI element**, verify it exists in the showcase. If it doesn't exist, create the component in `ui/` and document it in the showcase first, then use it.

### New UI Component Documentation Rule (MANDATORY)

**Whenever a new component is added to `nexacore-dashboard/src/components/ui/`**, the developer MUST also:

1. **Export a consolidated spec object** alongside the component (e.g., `myComponentSpecs`) — or split exports (`baseClass` + `variantClasses` + `sizeClasses`) when the component has many variants. The spec must use design tokens (semantic color/spacing/radius/shadow tokens), not raw hex/rgba/pixel values. If the component genuinely has no spec to export (rare — only for compound JSX-only components), document that explicitly in the §section using the "JSX-only" disclosure pattern.

2. **Add a corresponding §section in `ai-specs/specs/ui-design-system.md`** following the established template:
   - `### §N ComponentName` heading + 1-line description
   - **Properties** table (props + types + defaults + description)
   - Bold-headed sub-sections for behavior, variants, states, accessibility (use sub-headings sparingly — only when the component genuinely has multiple modes or primitives)
   - **Token references** section listing every token the component consumes
   - **Source** citation pointing to the `ui/*.tsx` file and exported spec
   - Cross-references to related §sections (bidirectional when applicable)
   - Closing `---` separator
   - Insertion point: before `## Common Patterns` for atoms; choose semantically grouped position for cluster additions

3. **Verify cross-reference text-match validation passes** doc-wide after the addition: every `§N Name` reference in the doc must match an actual heading. Run a sweep for the new component's name + its section number (and any abbreviated forms) before considering the doc change done.

4. **PR review checkpoint**: reviewers MUST confirm the new `ui/*.tsx` file has (a) a corresponding §section in `ui-design-system.md`, (b) an entry in `componentRegistry.ts` (either standalone OR bundled into a family entry like "Button" or "Feedback / Alerts"), AND (c) a `<ShowcaseSection>` block in `ComponentShowcase.tsx` (either dedicated OR a labeled demo inside the family section). PRs missing any of these should be blocked at review unless the addition is explicitly out-of-scope and tagged with a follow-up ticket.

5. **Update `componentRegistry.ts` and `<ShowcaseSection>` in `ComponentShowcase.tsx`**: every new `ui/*.tsx` file MUST be reachable from `/admin/design-system` catalog. Either (a) add a new entry to `componentRegistry` with category `atom` or `molecule` plus a corresponding `<ShowcaseSection title="...">` block, OR (b) expand an existing family entry (e.g., add `IconButton` to the "Button" entry's `files[]`) and ensure the family's `<ShowcaseSection>` includes a labeled demo for the new component. Update the `componentToSection` mapping in `app/admin/design-system/page.tsx` if a new standalone catalog entry is created. Verify the catalog card count in the rendered `/admin/design-system` page matches `componentRegistry.length`.

**Anti-patterns to avoid:**
- Adding a `ui/*.tsx` component without a §section (creates "Missing-from-doc" drift — the exact category SCRUM-329 Part B reconciled across 9 sub-tickets).
- Documenting a component with raw hex/rgba/pixel values instead of token references (creates "Drifted" rows — second-largest category in the SCRUM-329 audit).
- Documenting Common Patterns sub-sections that drift from canonical §sections (use cross-references, not duplicated specs).
- Embedding spec values directly in JSX without an exported spec object (makes future doc reconciliation impossible without re-reading JSX).
- Adding a `ui/*.tsx` component without a `componentRegistry` entry — component is invisible from `/admin/design-system` catalog and cannot be linked or discovered. (This was the exact 17% drift category SCRUM-348 fixed: AlertBox, IconButton, SegmentedControl, EmailSelector, StickyCard, ThemeToggle, TurnstileWidget, plus the broken Sidebar.tsx → SidebarNav.tsx file reference.)

**Reference**: see `ai-specs/specs/ui-design-system.md` §1-§50 for established section templates, and the SCRUM-329 audit deliverable at `ai-specs/changes/dashboard/audit/ui-design-system-2026-05-02/audit-table.md` for examples of every drift category this rule prevents.

### Component Conventions

#### Functional Components
- **Always use functional components** with hooks
- **Use TypeScript** for all components
- **Mark client components** with `'use client'` directive when they use hooks, event handlers, or browser APIs
- **Server Components** are the default in Next.js App Router — use them for static content

```typescript
// Client Component (uses hooks/events)
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';

interface ButtonProps extends React.ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> {
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'outline';
  loading?: boolean;
}

export default function Button({ variant = 'primary', loading, children, ...props }: ButtonProps) {
  const variantClasses = {
    primary: 'bg-accent text-white hover:bg-accent/90',
    secondary: 'bg-surface-secondary text-content-primary hover:bg-surface-secondary/80',
    outline: 'border border-border-default text-content-primary hover:bg-surface-secondary',
  };

  return (
    <button
      className={`w-full py-2.5 px-4 rounded-lg font-medium ${variantClasses[variant]} ${props.className || ''}`}
      disabled={loading || props.disabled}
      {...props}
    >
      {loading ? <Spinner /> : children}
    </button>
  );
}
```

**Domain Components**: Follow the same functional component pattern for domain-specific components. Organize by domain area (`components/projects/`, `components/teams/`, `components/billing/`, `components/notifications/`, `components/settings/`). Each domain component folder groups related components by feature.

#### Component Props
- **Define TypeScript interfaces** for component props
- Use **destructuring** for props
- **Extend HTML attributes** when wrapping native elements
- Include **default values** where appropriate

### State Management

#### Context + Reducer Pattern

For complex state (like authentication), use `useReducer` with typed actions inside a React Context provider.

```typescript
// context/AuthContext.tsx
'use client';

type AuthState = {
  user: SafeUser | null;
  accessToken: string | null;
  isLoading: boolean;
  error: string | null;
};

type AuthAction =
  | { type: 'AUTH_START' }
  | { type: 'AUTH_SUCCESS'; payload: { user: SafeUser; accessToken: string } }
  | { type: 'AUTH_ERROR'; payload: string }
  | { type: 'LOGOUT' }
  | { type: 'CLEAR_ERROR' };

function authReducer(state: AuthState, action: AuthAction): AuthState {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'AUTH_START':
      return { ...state, isLoading: true, error: null };
    case 'AUTH_SUCCESS':
      return { user: action.payload.user, accessToken: action.payload.accessToken, isLoading: false, error: null };
    case 'AUTH_ERROR':
      return { ...state, isLoading: false, error: action.payload };
    case 'LOGOUT':
      return { user: null, accessToken: null, isLoading: false, error: null };
    case 'CLEAR_ERROR':
      return { ...state, error: null };
    default:
      return state;
  }
}
```

**Additional contexts follow the same reducer pattern.** Keep contexts focused on a single domain area (auth, project, theme) to avoid unnecessary re-renders.

```typescript
// context/ProjectContext.tsx — manages selected project state
'use client';

type ProjectState = {
  currentProject: Project | null;
  projects: Project[];
  isLoading: boolean;
};

type ProjectAction =
  | { type: 'SET_PROJECTS'; payload: Project[] }
  | { type: 'SELECT_PROJECT'; payload: Project }
  | { type: 'CLEAR_PROJECT' };

// Consumed via useProject() hook
```

#### Local State with Hooks
- Use **useState** for component-level state
- Use **useEffect** for side effects and data fetching
- **Extract custom hooks** for reusable stateful logic

```typescript
const [formData, setFormData] = useState({ email: '', password: '' });

const handleInputChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
  const { name, value } = e.target;
  setFormData(prev => ({ ...prev, [name]: value }));
};
```

#### Loading and Error States
- **Always handle loading states** for async operations
- **Implement error handling** with user-friendly messages
- **Use the ErrorAlert component** for displaying errors

```typescript
const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);
const { error, clearError } = useAuth();

const handleSubmit = async () => {
  try {
    setIsLoading(true);
    clearError();
    await login(email, password);
  } catch {
    // Error is handled by AuthContext reducer
  } finally {
    setIsLoading(false);
  }
};
```

#### Error Message Detection Pattern
- **All backend error message detection MUST use constants from `src/lib/error-constants.ts`**. Never use inline string literals for behavior branching.
- **String matching** (`message.toLowerCase().includes(CONSTANT)`) is acceptable when `error.code` does not distinguish the specific business error. Always use centralized constants.
- **Status code overrides** (`extractMessageByStatus` from `src/lib/error-utils.ts`) for profile component error handling — pass a map of `statusCode → message`.
- **Shared helpers**: Use `extractErrorMessage` (for AuthContext) and `extractMessageByStatus` (for profile components) from `src/lib/error-utils.ts`. Do NOT create local `extractMessage` functions.
- **When the backend changes an error message**, update the corresponding constant in `error-constants.ts` — all detection logic propagates automatically.

### Service Layer Architecture

#### ApiClient Pattern
- **Singleton ApiClient** using `fetch` for HTTP requests
- **Automatic token management**: Bearer header injection
- **Token refresh on 401**: Silent refresh and retry failed requests, EXCEPT for unauthenticated auth endpoints (see "401 Routing Rule" below)
- **Structured error responses**: Parse JSON error bodies

```typescript
// lib/api.ts
class ApiClient {
  private accessToken: string | null = null;
  private refreshPromise: Promise<string | null> | null = null;

  setAccessToken(token: string | null) {
    this.accessToken = token;
  }

  async request<T>(endpoint: string, options: RequestInit = {}): Promise<T> {
    const headers: Record<string, string> = {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      ...(this.accessToken && { Authorization: `Bearer ${this.accessToken}` }),
    };

    const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}${endpoint}`, { ...options, headers });

    if (response.status === 401 && this.accessToken) {
      // Attempt silent token refresh
      const newToken = await this.silentRefresh();
      if (newToken) {
        // Retry original request with new token
        headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${newToken}`;
        const retryResponse = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}${endpoint}`, { ...options, headers });
        return retryResponse.json();
      }
      // Refresh failed — session is irrecoverable
      this.accessToken = null;
      this.onAuthFailure?.();  // Triggers LOGOUT in AuthContext
    }

    if (!response.ok) {
      const errorData = await response.json();
      throw errorData;
    }

    return response.json();
  }
}

export const apiClient = new ApiClient();

// Usage examples for different domain areas:
const projects = await apiClient.request<PaginatedResponse<Project>>('/projects?page=1&limit=10');
const project = await apiClient.request<Project>('/projects', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'New Project' }),
});
const notifications = await apiClient.request<PaginatedResponse<Notification>>('/notifications?status=UNREAD');
const subscription = await apiClient.request<Subscription>(`/projects/${projectId}/billing/subscription`);
```

#### 401 Routing Rule (MANDATORY)

`apiClient.request()` triggers silent token refresh on 401 responses ONLY for authenticated endpoints. Unauthenticated auth endpoints are listed in the module-level `SKIP_REFRESH_ON_401` set in `src/lib/api.ts`:

```typescript
const SKIP_REFRESH_ON_401 = new Set([
  "/auth/login",
  "/auth/register",
  "/auth/refresh",
  "/auth/forgot-password",
]);

if (response.status === 401 && !SKIP_REFRESH_ON_401.has(endpoint)) {
  // silentRefresh + retry
}
```

**Why**: a 401 from `/auth/login` means "Invalid credentials", not "expired session". If silentRefresh runs, it calls `/auth/refresh` → fails (no valid refresh cookie) → throws `SessionExpiredError`, swallowing the original "Invalid credentials" body. The user then sees "Sign in failed — An unexpected error occurred." instead of the security-compliant message, and a spurious "Session expired" toast.

**When adding a new endpoint**: if the endpoint is reachable BEFORE authentication AND can return 401 for credential reasons (not session reasons), add it to the set. Examples that belong in the set: any public auth flow start (`/auth/login`, `/auth/register`, `/auth/forgot-password`), the refresh endpoint itself (its 401 means refresh-token-invalid, not "try refresh again"). Examples that do NOT belong: `/auth/me`, `/users/me/*`, any endpoint guarded by `JwtAuthGuard`.

**Standards backed**:
- SCRUM-217 — anti-enumeration (CWE-204/203): same message regardless of failure cause
- SCRUM-284 — unified error response shape `{success:false, error:{message,code,statusCode}}`
- SCRUM-342 — fix the regression introduced when 401 routing was made unconditional

Removing or weakening this rule requires re-validating SCRUM-217/342 contracts (login fail UX + anti-enumeration tests).

#### Backend errors are toast-only (MANDATORY)

`AuthContext` callbacks (`login`, `register`, `forgotPassword`, `resetPassword`, MFA verify, etc.) MUST dispatch `AUTH_STOP` on backend failure — never `AUTH_ERROR`. The backend error message is delivered via `addToast({variant:"error", title:"...", description: extractErrorMessage(err)})` and that is the entire UX contract for server errors.

`<InlineError>` rendered below form inputs is reserved for **client-side form validation only** (e.g., "Enter a valid email", "Password too short", "Codes don't match"). It must never be populated from a server response, because:

- A failed `/auth/login` would otherwise show the SAME message twice (toast + inline) — visual noise.
- Persistent inline backend errors stay on screen even after the user starts retyping, which conflicts with `aria-live="polite"` semantics tuned for transient validation feedback.
- The convention is uniform across every auth form — making login an exception breaks user expectations.

The `activeError = formError || error` pattern present in some forms (`activeError = passwordError || error`, etc.) is defensive — the `|| error` half is never expected to fire in production. Do NOT introduce code that sets `state.error` from a server response.

#### API Route Handlers
- Use Next.js **Route Handlers** (`app/api/`) for server-side token management
- Store **refresh tokens** in secure httpOnly cookies (server-side only)
- Keep **access tokens** in memory only (never localStorage)

```typescript
// app/api/auth/refresh/route.ts
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const refreshToken = cookies().get('refreshToken')?.value;
  if (!refreshToken) return NextResponse.json({ error: 'No refresh token' }, { status: 401 });

  const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/auth/refresh`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ refreshToken }),
  });

  // Return new access token to client
}
```

